edwm - what was added, and how to check each piece
==================================================
Everything below is new on top of your dwm fork. Each item is independent, so
work down the list and stop wherever something misbehaves.

Nothing here changes your existing bindings: floating-by-default, taskview,
snapping, staylow, always-on-top, keyboard move/resize and the two-button
chord drag all work as they did. Item 0 is there to confirm that.

First, get the config in place:

    make clean install
    make install-config              # writes ~/.config/edwm, never overwrites
    cp theme.conf.sample ~/.config/edwm/theme.conf     # optional, more to look at

To test without leaving your current session, run edwm nested:

    Xephyr -screen 1280x800 -br -ac -noreset :9 &
    DISPLAY=:9 dwm &


0. Nothing regressed
--------------------
    * windows still open floating
    * Alt+Shift+] still quits
    * hold Button1 on a window, then press Button3 and drag -- the window
      moves (this is the chord gesture; it needs XInput2)
    * taskview, snapping and staylow behave as before

If any of this is wrong, stop -- the rest is built on it.


1. Runtime theming
------------------
The headline. No recompile, no restart, nothing lost.

    edwmctl theme list               what is installed
    edwmctl theme gruvbox            bar, borders and dmenu all change
    edwmctl theme nord
    edwmctl theme paper              a light one, to be sure it is not dark-only

Then edit the file directly and save it -- it reloads on save:

    $EDITOR ~/.config/edwm/theme.conf
      change   theme = nord     to    theme = dracula

Tone: retint the whole palette at once rather than editing colours.

    edwmctl tone hue=+40 saturation=1.3
    edwmctl tone hue=0 saturation=1 lightness=1 contrast=1 opacity=1   # reset

Fonts change live too, and the bar resizes with them. In theme.conf:

    [font]
    main = JetBrains Mono:size=14

Robustness -- put a deliberate typo in and save:

    [palette]
    accent = #NOTACOLOUR

The window manager must keep running, keep the previous theme, and print a
message naming the file and line. If it dies, that is a bug.


2. Taskbar
----------
The bar lists every window on the current tag, oldest first.

    * open three or four windows; each gets a button
    * the focused one is highlighted, and the count (#N) matches
    * Button1 on a button focuses and raises that window
    * Button1 on the button of the ALREADY focused window minimises it into
      the staylow corner stack, and its button dims
    * Button2 toggles always-on-top
    * Button3 closes the window
    * clicks on the tags, the layout symbol and the #N badge still do their
      own thing and are no longer mistaken for title clicks


3. System tray
--------------
Two incompatible kinds of applet, both handled. See "BSD tray" below for
what to actually run on FreeBSD.

    make test-clients
    DISPLAY=:9 ./tests/traytest '#ff0000' &     # XEmbed
    DISPLAY=:9 ./tests/traytest '#00ff00' &

    * both icons appear at the right of the bar
    * kill one; the tray shrinks and reflows with no gap
    * kill both; the status text expands into the space

    DISPLAY=:9 ./tests/traytest '#ffcc00' -t &

    * this one hides and shows itself every few seconds. It must come BACK
      each time. An icon that hides is not an icon that has quit, and
      treating it as one is the usual bug here.


4. StatusNotifierItem tray  (needs the libdbus build)
-----------------------------------------------------
Modern applets do not dock a window; they publish themselves on the bus.

    DISPLAY=:9 ./tests/snitest.py audio-volume-high &

Needs python3 dbus and gobject bindings (FreeBSD: py311-dbus py311-gobject3).

    * a speaker icon appears in the tray
    * ./tests/snitest.py audio-volume-high --cycle    changes icon and status
      on a timer; the icon must follow, and vanish while status is Passive
    * Button1 on it prints ACTIVATE in the script's output, Button2 SECONDARY,
      Button3 CONTEXTMENU


5. Applet menus  (needs the libdbus build)
------------------------------------------
Right click a StatusNotifierItem icon. edwm fetches the menu over DBusMenu
and draws it.

    * separators, greyed-out entries and checkmarks all render
    * entries marked invisible do not appear at all
    * submenus open on hover, and flip to the left when there is no room
    * Up/Down/Left/Right, Enter and Escape work
    * Escape inside a submenu goes up one level; again closes the menu
    * a click outside dismisses it
    * menu colours follow the theme (menu, menusel, menudim schemes)

Not implemented: keyboard shortcut labels, and icon-name lookup for menu
entries (only the inline icons applets send are drawn).


6. D-Bus session  (the part that fixes applications)
----------------------------------------------------
Start through the wrapper, which is what makes this work:

    exec edwm-session               # in ~/.xinitrc

Then check the session was described properly:

    echo $XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP       # edwm
    echo $DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS  # not empty

    * notify-send hello             shows something (needs a notification daemon)
    * a GTK file chooser opens promptly rather than hanging for ~25s
    * exactly ONE dbus-daemon for your session, not two

edwm never starts a bus of its own. If there is none it says so and tells you
to use edwm-session, which wraps the session in dbus-run-session.


7. The edwm D-Bus service  (needs the libdbus build)
----------------------------------------------------
FreeBSD has no busctl; use dbus-send, or gdbus if glib is installed.

    dbus-send --session --print-reply --dest=org.edwm.WM /org/edwm/WM \
        org.freedesktop.DBus.Introspectable.Introspect

    dbus-send --session --print-reply --dest=org.edwm.WM /org/edwm/WM \
        org.edwm.WM.GetTheme

    dbus-send --session --print-reply --dest=org.edwm.WM /org/edwm/WM \
        org.edwm.WM.SetTheme string:gruvbox

    dbus-send --session --print-reply --dest=org.edwm.WM /org/edwm/WM \
        org.edwm.WM.ListClients

Then the one that matters -- restart the bus and confirm edwm survives it,
reconnects, and keeps its name. It should not need restarting.


8. Control without D-Bus
------------------------
Build with DBUSLIBS and DBUSFLAGS commented out in config.mk. edwm loses the
service, the SNI tray and applet menus, but must still run and still theme:

    edwmctl theme nord              # falls back to the _EDWM_CMD root property
    kill -HUP $(pgrep -x dwm)       # also reloads


Testing the tray on FreeBSD
===========================
FreeBSD has no NetworkManager, so nm-applet -- the applet this was built
against -- will not be there. Use these instead.

Neutral first, with no applet at all:

    make test-clients
    ./tests/traytest '#ff0000' &

That is a pure XEmbed client and proves the XEmbed half works. For the
StatusNotifierItem half:

    pkg install py311-dbus py311-gobject3
    ./tests/snitest.py audio-volume-high --cycle &

Both test clients are self-contained and need no applet.

Real applets to try:

    pkg install networkmgr          # the usual FreeBSD network tray applet
    pkg install blueman             # bluetooth, if you have it
    pkg install pasystray           # pulseaudio volume

Whichever kind they are, they should appear: edwm hosts both protocols and
lays them out side by side.

To see which kind an applet is, check whether it took a bus name:

    dbus-send --session --print-reply --dest=org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher \
        /StatusNotifierWatcher org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties.Get \
        string:org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher \
        string:RegisteredStatusNotifierItems

A non-empty list means it is a StatusNotifierItem and is being hosted by
edwm. An empty list plus a visible icon means it used XEmbed.

If NO tray icons appear at all, in that order:

    * is another tray already running? edwm queues for the watcher name
      rather than stealing it, and says so on stderr. Only one tray per
      session.
    * was it built with libdbus? Without it the SNI half is absent, though
      XEmbed still works -- check with ./tests/traytest.
    * is there a session bus? echo $DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS


Known FreeBSD caveats
=====================
* Live theme reload on file-save uses kqueue there rather than inotify. It
  compiles and the rest of the BSD path was tested, but the watch itself
  could not be verified off-FreeBSD. If saving theme.conf does not reload,
  that is the thing to report -- edwmctl reload and SIGHUP are unaffected.
* Your config.h still points termcmd at qterminal, and the wordlist,
  webshell and backlight bindings reference paths that do not exist.
